WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:26.230
JEFFREY EISENACH: Well, welcome everyone.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:32.310
My name is Jeff Eisenach, and I’m a visiting
scholar here at the American Enterprise Institute.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:42.430
It’s a wonderful pleasure for us today to
welcome FCC Chairman Ajit Pai to the American

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.010
Enterprise Institute.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:49.710
Over the course — well, I’ve known Ajit
for some years, but over the course of the

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:55.319
past several months, I’ve had the opportunity
to get to know him better in my role as part

00:02:55.319 --> 00:03:00.290
of the Trump transition team for the Federal
Communications Commission.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:04.930
And when people ask me about that, I tell
them that of all of the various transition

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:14.170
teams for the Trump transition, I had the
lightest lift of all, and that’s because

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:21.260
we had these two wonderful Republican commissioners
present and in place at the FCC.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:26.420
And people would ask me, “What would the
Trump administration’s communications policy

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.011
agenda be after the inauguration?”

00:03:28.011 --> 00:03:30.630
And I said, “Well, don’t ask me.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:36.319
Go to the FCC website, and read the dissents
of Ajit Pai and Mike O’Rielly over the course

00:03:36.319 --> 00:03:41.389
of the past few years, and you’ll see it
spelled out with tremendous substance and

00:03:41.389 --> 00:03:43.549
detail.”

00:03:43.549 --> 00:03:53.060
I want to talk for just a second about Ajit’s
accomplishments over the past 102 days.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:58.810
Obviously, there’s some controversial issues
in front of the commission, but there are

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.850
some issues in front of the commission which
ought to be — at least ought to be less

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:11.340
controversial, and Ajit has taken those on
at a pace and with a level of substance and

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:20.940
success far more akin to an internet startup
than a typical Washington moribund bureaucracy.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:30.490
First of all, Ajit has put in place commonsense
procedural reforms like making notices, orders

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.180
public before they’re voted on by the commission.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.810
What a concept.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.540
You thought that could have happened before.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.430
Revoking the staff’s editorial privileges
so after the commission votes on something,

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:45.430
the staff now actually has to carry out what
the commission voted on instead of rewriting

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:50.370
it under historical editorial privileges.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:55.680
And many of these are bipartisan, including
another one being simply telling the staff

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.659
that when a commissioner, even a commissioner
from the other political party, asks you for

00:05:00.659 --> 00:05:04.620
information about a proceeding or something
going on at the commission, you should answer

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.000
the question.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:11.490
Now, you would think that’s all just good
government, but it is the antithesis of the

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:18.500
previous four years under the previous chairman,
who we won’t name here.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:23.940
Chairman Pai has also taken a weed-whacker
to the regulatory underbrush, little-noticed

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:30.789
changes — eliminating unnecessary paperwork
requirements and, at the other end of the

00:05:30.789 --> 00:05:36.039
spectrum, actually requiring that companies
applying for government subsidies demonstrate

00:05:36.039 --> 00:05:40.719
their bona fides to receive those subsidies
and spend them in a responsible way.

00:05:40.719 --> 00:05:42.740
What another concept.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:49.080
On the substantive front, it’s been change
after change after change, all of them in

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:59.800
the spirit of another FCC chairman, 17 years
ago, Chairman Bill Kennard who spoke eloquently

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:06.950
about the need to create an FCC for the 21st
century, to clean out the regulatory underbrush,

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:13.400
to move from a system of siloes and heavy-handed
ex ante regulation to a competition-based

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:18.760
framework, an enforcement-based framework
of ex-post regulation consistent with the

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:22.340
increasing competitiveness of the telecommunications
marketplace.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:30.140
And on issue after issue, Chairman Pai has
moved that agenda along, including the very

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:35.600
visible issues, which I’m sure we’ll get
into today, but also less visible issues,

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.890
something as important as beginning the process
of deregulating the marketplace for business

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:47.450
data services, which is essentially the internet
backbone services and, which is and has been

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:50.380
competitive — we’re finally going to get
around to deregulating that.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:55.780
And something as you might think mundane or
technical as beginning the process of permitting

00:06:55.780 --> 00:07:01.640
LTE-U into the marketplace, allowing new technologies
to be rolled out without the heavy hand of

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:03.909
government slowing innovation.

00:07:03.909 --> 00:07:05.360
So I’ve talked long enough.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.839
I’m not going to give a long introduction
of Ajit.

00:07:08.839 --> 00:07:12.450
He is currently chairman of the Federal Communications
Commission.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:16.700
Previously, he was a member of the Federal
Communications Commission.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:22.630
Prior to that, he served in the General Counsel’s
Office at the Federal Communications Commission.

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:27.210
His dedication to the agency I think is an
important part of his success.

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:31.820
And it’s worth, I think, going back and
looking at his first speech as chairman, which

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:41.020
was to the staff of the agency, reassuring
them that his purpose is to advance the agency

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:45.750
and its professional staff, not to attack
it.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:50.930
And I think that’s been another component
of his chairmanship that’s been so important.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:55.520
Lest we forget, Ajit graduated from Harvard
University.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:00.310
He received his law degree from the University
of Chicago, where he was editor of the law

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:08.850
review, and so we are dealing not only with
a great leader but a tremendous intellect.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:15.470
Ajit Pai, thank you for being here.

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:16.470
(Applause.)

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:17.910
AJIT PAI: Thank you, Jeff, for those very
kind words.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:22.270
Thanks to the entire team at AEI for putting
this event together.

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:27.589
Jeff, I especially appreciate the comparison
to former Chairman Bill Kennard, distinguished

00:08:27.589 --> 00:08:31.540
public servant and someone I can now call
a friend.

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:37.149
To be mentioned even in the same sentence
as Chairman Kennard is a high honor indeed.

00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:41.490
It is great to be back here at the American
Enterprise Institute.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:47.560
Being here today reminds me that 2016 was
a time of great change in Washington.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:52.830
Looking back, it’s clear that we witnessed
a momentous event, one that I’m sure caused

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:58.450
many people some consternation, brought tremendous
joy to others, and required a lot of people

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:01.920
to pack up their offices and move out.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.570
I’m referring, of course, to AEI’s decision
to move into this wonderful new space after

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.820
many years on 17th Street.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.790
Congratulations on the move.

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:16.990
But in all seriousness, AEI and its scholars
have done tremendous work by making the case

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:23.170
for expanding freedom, for extending individual
opportunity, and for strengthening America’s

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:24.450
free enterprise system.

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:29.220
And in particular, AEI has shown thought leadership
on communications policy.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:34.250
And much of the credit for that goes to Jeffrey
Eisenach, who has really done so much work

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:37.350
over the years, including during the transition
that he mentioned.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:41.649
So, thank you, Jeff, for your scholarship,
for your friendship, and for your counsel.

00:09:41.649 --> 00:09:48.880
Now, earlier this week, as Jeff pointed out,
we reached the 100-day mark of my tenure as

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:49.880
chairman.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:52.760
It sure doesn’t feel like 100 days, I have
to tell you.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:56.649
Time flies much more quickly in this job than
it did when I was in the minority.

00:09:56.649 --> 00:10:01.420
But, by any objective measure, we’ve hit
the ground running, and we have had a very

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:04.370
productive three-plus months at the FCC.

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:08.770
Here’s just one data point for your consideration.

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:12.350
In my first 100 days, the FCC adopted 49 items.

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:17.510
To put that number in perspective, during
the prior two permanent chairmen’s first

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:21.600
100 days, the commission adopted 25 and 34
items.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.329
I know what you’re thinking.

00:10:24.329 --> 00:10:28.550
You know, as legendary UCLA basketball coach
John Wooden once said, never mistake activity

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:30.500
for achievement.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:34.300
So I thought it would be helpful this morning
to highlight some of the actions we’ve taken

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:38.090
and to explain how they fit into a broader
strategic vision.

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:43.010
And that vision includes five goals that are
important to me and, hopefully, to you.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:45.710
Number one, closing the digital divide.

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:47.760
Number two, modernizing our rules.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:49.840
Number three, promoting innovation.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:53.750
Number four, protecting consumers and public
safety.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:56.770
And number five, improving the agency’s
operations.

00:10:56.770 --> 00:11:01.530
Now, these are the goals that I’ve emphasized
since I arrived at the FCC almost exactly

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.050
five years ago today.

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:09.320
These are the goals that have shaped and animated
my first 100 days as chairman, and these are

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:13.630
the goals that I hope will be the agency’s
beacon in the years to come.

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:16.980
So let’s start with closing the digital
divide.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:21.720
On my first full day as chairman, as Jeff
pointed out, I delivered remarks to the agency’s

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.570
fantastic and talented staff.

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:28.570
And I focused on one issue: bringing the benefits
of the digital age to all Americans.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:32.720
And this principle goes back to the commission’s
founding statute.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:37.860
Section One of the Communications Act of 1934
charged the FCC with making communications

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:44.120
services available, so far as possible, to
all the people of the United States.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:48.390
And to me at least, this mission is more vital
than ever.

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:54.110
After all, broadband opens doors to opportunity
in almost every aspect of modern American

00:11:54.110 --> 00:12:00.030
life, whether it’s looking for a job, getting
medical care, doing your homework, keeping

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:05.160
in touch with friends and family, or engaging
with your favorite regulators.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:08.940
That was a joke actually.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.960
I’m here all week.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:16.769
Broadband has also given rise to what I’ve
called the democratization of entrepreneurship.

00:12:16.769 --> 00:12:21.650
With a powerful plan and a digital connection,
you can raise capital, you can start a business,

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:27.750
you can hire people, and you could reach a
global customer base from anywhere in America.

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:32.530
And so I believe that every American who wants
to participate in our digital economy should

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:34.890
be able to do so.

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:39.930
Access to digital opportunity should not depend
on who you are, where you live, what you look

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.830
like, or where you’re from.

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:45.040
But, to be clear, we’re not where we need
to be.

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:50.010
If you live in rural America, you are much
less likely to have high-speed internet access

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.110
than if you live in a city.

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:55.790
If you live in a low-income neighborhood,
you are much less likely to have high-speed

00:12:55.790 --> 00:12:59.670
internet access than if you live in a wealthier
area.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:03.770
The digital divide in our country is real,
and it is persistent.

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:08.930
It makes it harder for many of our fellow
American citizens to improve their lives,

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:14.560
and it takes a significant toll on America’s
economy and our global competitiveness.

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:19.700
When I became chairman, the FCC needed to
step up its efforts to close this gap.

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:22.770
And that is exactly what we’ve done.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:27.450
My first two meetings as FCC Chairman involved
conversations with consumer advocates and

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:31.800
small broadband providers on ways to promote
digital empowerment.

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:37.150
My very first vote as chairman was to partner
with the state of New York to deliver $170

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:41.690
million for broadband deployment in upstate
areas where residents have been bypassed for

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:44.140
far too long.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:48.240
And the first commission meeting for which
I had the power to set the agenda featured

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:53.190
two significant measures to expand broadband
access in unserved areas.

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:57.800
One was an order, passed unanimously, to bring
mobile broadband to millions of Americans

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:00.940
through what is known as the Mobility Fund
Phase Two.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:06.900
Previously, the FCC was spending about $25
million a month of taxpayer money to subsidize

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:12.190
wireless carriers in areas where private capital
was already doing the job.

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:17.870
We are redirecting that spending and more
— $4.53 billion over the next decade — in

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:23.230
order to bring 4G LTE service to rural Americans
who don’t have it today.

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:28.370
And we’re doing it in an efficient, fiscally
responsible way by using a competitive reverse

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:31.040
auction to allocate those funds.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:36.130
Now, at the same meeting we decided — again,
on a bipartisan basis — to move forward

00:14:36.130 --> 00:14:41.110
with $2 billion in wired broadband investment
through what’s called Phase Two of our Connect

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:42.300
America Fund.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:47.060
Here too, we set up a competitive bidding
process to get the most bang for the buck

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:52.460
in terms of bringing high-speed internet access
to currently unserved rural Americans.

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:57.040
Our rules for this auction will encourage
a wide range of entities to compete and to

00:14:57.040 --> 00:15:01.750
participate, from wireless internet service
providers to electric utilities.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:08.150
Now, these two capital investment plans will
bring internet access to many unserved Americans.

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:11.920
And they will put many Americans to work,
building next-generation networks in rural

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:13.410
America.

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:16.360
But these were just parts of the opening act.

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:20.880
Our most recent FCC meeting included three
initiatives designed to make it easier for

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:24.830
companies to build and expand high-speed broadband
networks.

00:15:24.830 --> 00:15:30.270
We launched, again unanimously, a proceeding
to lower costs and speed the deployment of

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:35.860
new wireline infrastructure, to speed the
transition from copper networks to modern

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:37.830
fiber networks.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:42.190
This means that more money will be spent on
the resilient technologies of tomorrow, not

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:45.080
the fading networks of yesterday.

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:49.750
And we launched, yet again unanimously, a
proceeding to facilitate the deployment of

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:55.880
the hundreds of thousands of small cells that
will power the 5G networks of tomorrow.

00:15:55.880 --> 00:16:02.089
And we eliminated, still yet again unanimously,
a rule that needlessly penalized rural carriers,

00:16:02.089 --> 00:16:07.590
telephone companies for deploying broadband
to certain parts of their communities.

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:12.089
Looking ahead, we also want to explore fresh
ideas for tackling the long-standing challenge

00:16:12.089 --> 00:16:13.990
of this digital divide.

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:18.370
And to that end, we created the Broadband
Deployment Advisory Committee, a panel of

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:23.750
experts to make recommendations to the FCC
about strategies to promote better and faster

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:26.220
and cheaper broadband.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:32.800
Over 380 individuals applied to serve on this
committee, representing community groups,

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:38.089
community organizations and consumer organizations,
government, and industry — a diverse and

00:16:38.089 --> 00:16:41.240
highly impressive pool of applicants.

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:46.600
We recently invited 29 individuals to serve
on this committee, led by the highly talented

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:52.650
Elizabeth Pierce, the CEO of Quintillion Networks,
and Kelleigh Cole of the Utah Broadband Outreach

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:53.980
Center.

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:58.620
The BDAC, as we call it, held its first meeting
last month, and I expect it will make a major

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:03.100
difference in our work to connect all Americans.

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:06.189
Some of the actions we’ve taken to spur
broadband deployment and to close the digital

00:17:06.189 --> 00:17:11.870
divide dovetail with our second major priority,
which is modernizing the commission’s rules

00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:15.540
and eliminating unnecessary regulatory burdens.

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:20.049
You often hear talk about how regulatory red
tape imposes real costs and stifles investment

00:17:20.049 --> 00:17:21.380
and innovation.

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:26.390
Here’s one statistic that gives you a sense
of how real and how significant those costs

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:27.390
are.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:33.230
According to the Office of Management and
Budget, the FCC imposes $800 million each

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:35.809
and every year in paperwork burdens.

00:17:35.809 --> 00:17:39.730
That’s right: $800 million each year.

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:43.210
This doesn’t count the money that people
must spend paying workers to fill out all

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:44.299
of this paperwork.

00:17:44.299 --> 00:17:48.850
And, again, these are only the FCC’s paperwork
burdens that I’m talking about.

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:53.710
The total regulatory burden that the FCC places
on the private sector is actually much higher

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:55.360
than that.

00:17:55.360 --> 00:17:59.669
To me at least, the fundamental question is
this: Do we want that money to be diverted

00:17:59.669 --> 00:18:04.809
to lawyers and accountants figuring out how
to comply with FCC’s regulator requirements,

00:18:04.809 --> 00:18:10.539
or do we want it to be spent delivering American
consumers better and faster and cheaper internet

00:18:10.539 --> 00:18:12.039
access?

00:18:12.039 --> 00:18:15.890
No offense to my friends in the communications
bar, but to me at least, the answer to that

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:18.370
question is pretty clear.

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:23.500
Now, paperwork aside, we are reviewing the
FCC’s rules across the board and deciding

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:26.630
which ones still make sense in the digital
age.

00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:31.150
As part of this review, we’re asking whether
the costs of a rule outweigh the benefits.

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:37.049
When the facts warrant, we will not hesitate
to revise overly burdensome rules or to repeal

00:18:37.049 --> 00:18:38.049
them altogether.

00:18:38.049 --> 00:18:43.399
You don’t need a weatherman to know that
the wind is blowing certain FCC rules toward

00:18:43.399 --> 00:18:45.630
modification or elimination.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:49.590
And that’s especially true, by the way,
for small businesses.

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:53.240
Federal regulations have a disproportionate
effect on these companies.

00:18:53.240 --> 00:18:57.419
And these companies, remember, are often the
linchpin to a more competitive marketplace,

00:18:57.419 --> 00:19:02.200
and they don’t have the compliance resources
to withstand a regulatory onslaught.

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:06.960
I’m very sensitive to the impact that our
regulations can have on such businesses.

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:11.669
And that’s why, in my first week as chairman,
I proposed to relieve small internet service

00:19:11.669 --> 00:19:16.330
providers from costly and overly burdensome
reporting requirements associated with the

00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:20.840
Title Two order, and we did that in February.

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:26.309
This is just one example of many that illustrate
how we are modernizing our rules.

00:19:26.309 --> 00:19:31.279
We also relaxed unnecessary command-and-control
regulations for business data services to

00:19:31.279 --> 00:19:36.330
create greater incentives for the private
sector to invest in next-generation networks.

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:40.799
We streamlined various accounting requirements
for many wireline carriers, requirements that

00:19:40.799 --> 00:19:45.489
literally required them to maintain two different
sets of books.

00:19:45.489 --> 00:19:50.899
We eased reporting burdens for volunteer board
members of noncommercial broadcasters.

00:19:50.899 --> 00:19:55.200
We updated our interpretation of our equal
employment opportunity rules to account for

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:58.380
the ways that people actually look for jobs
today.

00:19:58.380 --> 00:20:02.869
And we eliminated two filing requirements:
broadcasters’ public correspondence files

00:20:02.869 --> 00:20:07.789
and cable operators’ principal headend locations
that no longer made any sense.

00:20:07.789 --> 00:20:12.999
Now, I recognize that as I go through this
litany, these decisions and others like them

00:20:12.999 --> 00:20:15.129
might seem like small potatoes.

00:20:15.129 --> 00:20:20.649
But remember what former Senate Minority Leader
Everett Dirksen once had to say about spending:

00:20:20.649 --> 00:20:26.419
a billion here, a billion there, and pretty
soon you are talking real money.

00:20:26.419 --> 00:20:32.989
So, too, as we go about eliminating a rule
here and a rule there, we will end up substantially

00:20:32.989 --> 00:20:36.929
adjusting our rules to match the realities
of the modern marketplace, and that is something

00:20:36.929 --> 00:20:40.440
that is good for consumers and good for companies
alike.

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:46.669
A third key FCC priority I mentioned is promoting
innovation across the communications industry.

00:20:46.669 --> 00:20:51.200
I might be new to the chairmanship, but I’ve
served on the commission, as I said, for almost

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:52.840
half a decade.

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:57.549
During that time, I have had the great privilege
of traveling all across this country and to

00:20:57.549 --> 00:20:59.309
all corners of the land.

00:20:59.309 --> 00:21:05.789
And I can report firsthand that the entrepreneurial
spirit is alive and well in America.

00:21:05.789 --> 00:21:11.139
Just this March, I took my first road trip
as chairman, visiting Pittsburgh and Youngstown

00:21:11.139 --> 00:21:13.259
and Cleveland and Detroit.

00:21:13.259 --> 00:21:19.240
Along the way, I met with an incredible array
of startups: a mobile app company that is

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:26.320
already helping 80 million people around the
world learn new languages for free, a virtual

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:32.299
reality gaming entrepreneur, a new online
platform to provide treatment for people with

00:21:32.299 --> 00:21:34.610
addiction, and many, many more like them.

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:40.110
I also visited established entities like the
Cleveland Clinic, where I witnessed a demonstration

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:45.159
of a mobile stroke unit that is using new
technology to cut the time needed to assess

00:21:45.159 --> 00:21:51.330
and stabilize a stroke patient by an average
of 38 minutes, critical minutes when you consider

00:21:51.330 --> 00:21:57.210
that the brain for a stroke patient loses
two million brain cells every single minute.

00:21:57.210 --> 00:22:02.580
Whether it’s someone tinkering in a small-town
garage or a growing company in a major technology

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:08.390
hub, we at the FCC want to help American entrepreneurs
bring these big ideas to life.

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:13.519
And that’s why we’ve put in place a process
to implement, at long last, Section Seven

00:22:13.519 --> 00:22:17.190
of the Communications Act, a long-neglected
law.

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:22.529
Going forward, if an innovator or an inventor
files a petition or application at the FCC

00:22:22.529 --> 00:22:27.600
for the approval of a new technology or service,
we will abide by the law, and we will make

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:29.330
a decision within one year.

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:33.110
There will be no more waiting indefinitely
for an answer.

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:37.619
As the old saying goes, tell me yes, tell
me no, or just tell me something.

00:22:37.619 --> 00:22:42.669
Now, that’s why we’ve changed our experimental
licensing program and we’ve launched a new

00:22:42.669 --> 00:22:47.600
website to make it easier for parties to register
proposed new experiments.

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:51.610
This new type of experimental license will
allow greater flexibility for parties, including

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:56.419
universities and research labs and health
care facilities and manufacturers of radio

00:22:56.419 --> 00:23:02.080
frequency equipment, to develop their technologies
and services while protecting incumbent services

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:03.639
against harmful interference.

00:23:03.639 --> 00:23:10.009
That’s why, as Jeff pointed out, we authorized
the first-ever LTE-unlicensed devices in the

00:23:10.009 --> 00:23:16.240
five gigahertz band, a significant advance
for wireless innovation and spectrum sharing.

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:21.070
This gives wireless consumers the best of
both worlds: more robust and seamless networks

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:27.710
when their phones are relying on cellular
networks and the continued enjoyment of Wi-Fi.

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:31.590
And that’s why we began the process of allowing
television broadcasters to fully enter the

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:38.269
digital age by using the next-generation television
standard on a voluntary, market-driven basis.

00:23:38.269 --> 00:23:43.889
This new transmission standard, known as ATSC
3.0, is the first one to marry the advantages

00:23:43.889 --> 00:23:46.480
of broadcasting and the internet.

00:23:46.480 --> 00:23:52.249
And it could let broadcasters offer much better
services in various ways, 4K transmissions,

00:23:52.249 --> 00:23:57.100
immersive audio, better accessibility options,
and much, much more.

00:23:57.100 --> 00:24:01.429
Now, one more point about our approach to
promoting innovation.

00:24:01.429 --> 00:24:05.870
We want to encourage innovation throughout
the internet economy.

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:10.289
That means innovation not just at the edge
of the networks, but also innovation at the

00:24:10.289 --> 00:24:13.020
core of those networks, within the networks
themselves.

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:18.570
Also, we want to encourage business-model
innovation and more options for consumers.

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:25.169
That’s a big reason why we closed the FCC’s
investigation into free-data offerings.

00:24:25.169 --> 00:24:30.830
Wireless companies offering consumers something
for free has, shockingly, proven popular among

00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:33.919
consumers, especially low-income Americans.

00:24:33.919 --> 00:24:37.850
And it’s also made the wireless marketplace
more competitive than ever.

00:24:37.850 --> 00:24:44.639
Going forward, the FCC will seek to promote,
not to prevent, that kind of innovation.

00:24:44.639 --> 00:24:49.429
And that leads to our fourth guiding priority,
which is protecting consumers, and that goes

00:24:49.429 --> 00:24:51.899
hand-in-hand with promoting public safety.

00:24:51.899 --> 00:24:56.299
The most effective tool for protecting consumers
is a competitive marketplace.

00:24:56.299 --> 00:25:01.529
But as we’ve seen, competition sometimes
isn’t enough to guard consumers against

00:25:01.529 --> 00:25:04.100
scams and other unsavory practices.

00:25:04.100 --> 00:25:09.009
When consumers feel they are being taking
advantage of, they often turn to the FCC for

00:25:09.009 --> 00:25:10.029
help.

00:25:10.029 --> 00:25:13.320
And this FCC is taking action.

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:19.460
The most number-one source of consumer complaints
at the FCC for many years has been robocalls.

00:25:19.460 --> 00:25:23.450
We’ve all experienced them during our daily
lives.

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:27.809
And here, the agency has moved aggressively
to save Americans from the scourge of Rachel

00:25:27.809 --> 00:25:31.940
from cardholder services and many of her friends.

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:36.679
We have sought to give providers more leeway
to block calls that used a spoofed caller

00:25:36.679 --> 00:25:41.580
ID number and that seemed to be from an unassigned
or invalid phone number.

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:45.600
This would impede the illegitimate callers
and scam artists who often engage in call

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:50.330
spoofing and prey upon some of our most vulnerable
populations.

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:55.659
We have also taken action to protect consumers,
many of whom are small businesses, from scams.

00:25:55.659 --> 00:26:00.710
For instance, we levied a $1 million fine
against a Florida-based long-distance carrier

00:26:00.710 --> 00:26:06.200
for impersonating representatives of customers’
existing long-distance providers and illegally

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:10.559
switching the customers’ long-distance carriers.

00:26:10.559 --> 00:26:15.039
Another area where this FCC has made a mark
is in ensuring that communications services

00:26:15.039 --> 00:26:18.779
are accessible to Americans with disabilities.

00:26:18.779 --> 00:26:22.720
For instance, we’ve already taken steps
to improve the quality and the efficiency

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:24.820
of video relay services.

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:28.729
These are services that help people who are
deaf, hard-of-hearing, or speech disabled

00:26:28.729 --> 00:26:29.899
communicate.

00:26:29.899 --> 00:26:35.059
Our reforms will encourage more competition,
and they will help make these services more

00:26:35.059 --> 00:26:40.909
useful and meaningful to disabled Americans
in their daily lives.

00:26:40.909 --> 00:26:43.480
Public safety is another dimension of consumer
protection.

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:48.100
Again, this is a principle that is rooted
in our founding statute, which charges the

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:53.249
FCC with protecting the safety of life and
property through communications.

00:26:53.249 --> 00:26:58.970
One area in which we delivered results involves
the problem of contraband cell phones.

00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:02.779
These devices are being smuggled into prisons
and jails across the country.

00:27:02.779 --> 00:27:07.659
I’ve seen it for myself in minimum security
facilities in Massachusetts, to maximum security

00:27:07.659 --> 00:27:09.230
facilities in Georgia.

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:14.979
Now, these devices are smuggled into prisons
and jails to be used for hits on witnesses,

00:27:14.979 --> 00:27:20.190
scams on innocent consumers, and many other
illicit purposes, all of which impact guards

00:27:20.190 --> 00:27:22.299
and inmates and all of us ultimately.

00:27:22.299 --> 00:27:28.629
I’m proud to say that here, the FCC unanimously
adopted reforms that will enable correctional

00:27:28.629 --> 00:27:33.000
facilities to detect and to block the use
of these contraband cell phones.

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:38.059
We’re also looking at other technological
solutions going forward.

00:27:38.059 --> 00:27:42.830
Outside of the rulemaking process, we also
rushed to the aid of Jewish Community Centers

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:47.950
and law enforcement to help combat a wave
of bomb threats earlier this year.

00:27:47.950 --> 00:27:53.739
And when a major 911 outage hit a couple of
months ago, we immediately launched an investigation

00:27:53.739 --> 00:27:59.260
to figure out what had happened and what could
be done to prevent it from happening again.

00:27:59.260 --> 00:28:05.179
Now, the last priority that I would like to
highlight is enhancing transparency and improving

00:28:05.179 --> 00:28:07.940
the way the agency does business.

00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:12.399
Long before I became chairman, I said that
good process drives good policy.

00:28:12.399 --> 00:28:17.460
As you may have heard back then, I had some
problems with FCC processes.

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:22.109
One of my favorite targets was the practice
of preventing the public from seeing a proposal

00:28:22.109 --> 00:28:27.250
or order until after the FCC voted on it.

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:34.090
I was told for many years it was unlawful
to change course or unwise to do so.

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:39.490
But having spent almost five years talking
about ways we could do things better, I was

00:28:39.490 --> 00:28:43.590
eager to begin doing things differently, and
we did.

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:48.470
In the category of they said it couldn’t
be done, we started making the text of items

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:53.889
available to the public on the internet three
weeks in advance of commission meetings.

00:28:53.889 --> 00:28:58.330
Of all the things that we’ve done over our
first 100 days, that is one of the initiatives

00:28:58.330 --> 00:29:00.460
that I’ve heard the most positive feedback
about.

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:05.669
Indeed, now that we let the sunlight in, it’s
hard to remember why this was even a controversial

00:29:05.669 --> 00:29:07.479
proposal in the first place.

00:29:07.479 --> 00:29:12.070
And to me at least, this is a useful reminder
of two things.

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:16.750
Number one, never underestimate the power
of government inertia.

00:29:16.750 --> 00:29:21.010
Number two, the most common defense for a
long-standing practice is “That’s just

00:29:21.010 --> 00:29:22.050
the way we’ve always done it.”

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:28.369
It might be a signal to think outside the
box and to begin doing things differently.

00:29:28.369 --> 00:29:32.080
Speaking of doing things differently, I have
long been concerned that the economists at

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:37.309
the agency haven not been systematically incorporated
into the FCC’s policy work.

00:29:37.309 --> 00:29:42.749
Instead, their expertise is typically applied
in an ad hoc fashion and often late into the

00:29:42.749 --> 00:29:43.749
process.

00:29:43.749 --> 00:29:48.470
Moreover, the economists are sprinkled among
the agency’s many bureaus and offices.

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:51.259
We are taking a major step toward correcting
that.

00:29:51.259 --> 00:29:57.190
A month ago, I kick-started a process to establish
an Office of Economics and Data.

00:29:57.190 --> 00:30:01.729
This office will combine economists and data
professionals from around the FCC.

00:30:01.729 --> 00:30:07.869
And I envision it providing economic analysis
for rulemakings, transactions, and auctions;

00:30:07.869 --> 00:30:11.879
managing the commission’s data resources;
and conducting longer-term research — those

00:30:11.879 --> 00:30:16.859
famed white papers, for instance — on ways
to improve the commission’s long-term policies.

00:30:16.859 --> 00:30:21.940
Now, my goal is to have the new office up
and running by the end of the year.

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:26.640
And I’d be remiss if I didn’t acknowledge
the prior work done by Jeff Eisenach, by Ross

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:30.799
Layton, Mark Jamison, and many others for
providing the intellectual foundation for

00:30:30.799 --> 00:30:33.700
creating this office.

00:30:33.700 --> 00:30:37.809
So there you have it: a recap of our first
100 days.

00:30:37.809 --> 00:30:40.289
Obviously, this is not an exhaustive list.

00:30:40.289 --> 00:30:46.049
For example, I mentioned neither the FCC’s
incentive auction, nor my infamous Reese’s

00:30:46.049 --> 00:30:47.669
peanut butter mug.

00:30:47.669 --> 00:30:53.019
But I hope I’ve illustrated at least in
brief how we intend to meet the core priorities

00:30:53.019 --> 00:31:00.120
of this agency: closing the digital divide,
modernizing our rules, promoting innovation,

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:03.830
protecting consumers and public safety, and
improving the agency’s operations.

00:31:03.830 --> 00:31:09.080
Now, it’s also important to note that our
accomplishments over the first 100 days were

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:12.489
truly a team effort.

00:31:12.489 --> 00:31:17.249
Commissioners Mignon Clyburn and Mike O’Rielly
have been critical partners in the commission’s

00:31:17.249 --> 00:31:18.249
work.

00:31:18.249 --> 00:31:22.159
For example, when it comes to closing the
digital divide, Commissioner Clyburn has played

00:31:22.159 --> 00:31:25.590
a vital role and has offered many productive
suggestions.

00:31:25.590 --> 00:31:31.210
Commissioner O’Rielly has been a leader
when it comes to process reform and to easing

00:31:31.210 --> 00:31:32.700
unnecessary regulatory burdens.

00:31:32.700 --> 00:31:35.409
And I could have no better partner than him.

00:31:35.409 --> 00:31:39.979
So I would like to take this opportunity to
publicly thank each of them for the work they

00:31:39.979 --> 00:31:42.299
have done over the past several months.

00:31:42.299 --> 00:31:48.009
And in those rare instances when we have disagreed,
the exchange of our views has made for a better

00:31:48.009 --> 00:31:50.499
ultimate work product.

00:31:50.499 --> 00:31:56.619
And of course, I must thank the unsung heroes
of the FCC, our amazing staff.

00:31:56.619 --> 00:32:01.049
None of what we have accomplished so far would
have been possible without their efforts.

00:32:01.049 --> 00:32:06.809
I am well aware that many of them have worked
very long hours over the last 100 days, often

00:32:06.809 --> 00:32:12.269
under very tight deadlines, but their work
has been stellar, their demeanor has been

00:32:12.269 --> 00:32:16.309
professional, and their dedication to the
cause has been unflagging.

00:32:16.309 --> 00:32:20.509
And so I appreciate all of the sacrifices
that they have made in order to advance the

00:32:20.509 --> 00:32:21.769
public interest.

00:32:21.769 --> 00:32:26.359
Now, as for me, I’m of course pleased that
we’ve gotten off to a fast start.

00:32:26.359 --> 00:32:30.009
But I would stress that that’s all it is:
just a start.

00:32:30.009 --> 00:32:33.200
A good beginning, of course, is no guarantee
of ultimate success.

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:40.470
If you don’t believe me, ask the 2017 Atlanta
Falcons, the 2016 Golden State Warriors, the

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:43.690
1986 Red Sox, and many other teams.

00:32:43.690 --> 00:32:46.779
So there’s a lot more to do in these fields.

00:32:46.779 --> 00:32:50.409
And I’m looking forward to doing it, to
building a brighter digital future for all

00:32:50.409 --> 00:32:52.059
Americans.

00:32:52.059 --> 00:32:53.609
Thank you very much for your attention.

00:32:53.609 --> 00:32:55.510
Thanks again to AEI for hosting us.

00:32:55.510 --> 00:33:00.169
And I look forward to continuing to work with
you in the years to come.

00:33:00.169 --> 00:33:02.249
(Applause.)

00:33:02.249 --> 00:33:04.330
MR.

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:13.960
EISENACH: So we’re going to engage in a
conversation here for just a couple of minutes,

00:33:13.960 --> 00:33:17.019
and then we’ll turn the floor open to questions.

00:33:17.019 --> 00:33:21.879
So one topic you didn’t talk about on your
speech I’ll go ahead and raise just so it

00:33:21.879 --> 00:33:22.879
doesn’t go unmentioned.

00:33:22.879 --> 00:33:27.129
I bet we get a question or two about it also,
and that is the Open Internet proceeding.

00:33:27.129 --> 00:33:28.129
MR.

00:33:28.129 --> 00:33:29.129
PAI: I’m not familiar with that.

00:33:29.129 --> 00:33:30.299
Is that a big deal?

00:33:30.299 --> 00:33:31.299
(Laughs.)

00:33:31.299 --> 00:33:32.299
MR.

00:33:32.299 --> 00:33:34.219
EISENACH: I’ve read something about that
recently.

00:33:34.219 --> 00:33:38.809
So, you know, without kind of getting deep
into the substance of the thing again, you

00:33:38.809 --> 00:33:43.919
know, you’ve written about that at length,
and you know, I think the arguments are well

00:33:43.919 --> 00:33:44.919
understood.

00:33:44.919 --> 00:33:52.590
But maybe looking ahead at the process, first
of all, the process at the FCC, the last time

00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:55.940
this issue came around was criticized.

00:33:55.940 --> 00:34:01.619
So maybe speak a little bit about the process
as you see it and the process reforms and

00:34:01.619 --> 00:34:03.990
how they play into the FCC’s deliberations.

00:34:03.990 --> 00:34:09.340
But then also, how do you see the role of
Congress, how do you see the role of the courts

00:34:09.340 --> 00:34:10.730
in resolving, you know, what’s obviously
a contentious issue?

00:34:10.730 --> 00:34:11.730
MR.

00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:12.730
PAI: A great question.

00:34:12.730 --> 00:34:18.350
So, first and foremost, we want to make sure
that this is a proceeding that respects the

00:34:18.350 --> 00:34:21.960
Administrative Procedure Act and sort of basic
principles of good government.

00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:26.399
And that’s why it was really important to
me that the pilot project that I announced

00:34:26.399 --> 00:34:32.570
in my second week in office extended to all
of the proposals that we would be considering,

00:34:32.570 --> 00:34:33.909
including this one.

00:34:33.909 --> 00:34:38.740
And so that’s why, after I made a speech
where I outlined my proposal to reform our

00:34:38.740 --> 00:34:41.879
internet regulations, I wanted to put it up
on the internet immediately.

00:34:41.879 --> 00:34:46.240
And, as I said at the time, you may disagree
with what I proposed, you may agree with it.

00:34:46.240 --> 00:34:51.409
But at least you’ll be able to see exactly
what it was that I was thinking and why.

00:34:51.409 --> 00:34:55.510
And that’s really important to me because
I don’t claim to have a monopoly on wisdom.

00:34:55.510 --> 00:34:59.150
I think it’s important for the public to
feel like they have a seat at the table.

00:34:59.150 --> 00:35:03.350
And for the FCC to simply say, “This is
our plan, just trust us, this is what’s

00:35:03.350 --> 00:35:07.020
in it” is not good enough for me as what
happened in the last iteration.

00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:10.530
And so going forward, we’re going to have
a very open and transparent process following

00:35:10.530 --> 00:35:13.810
on May 18 of the notice of proposed rulemaking
is adopted.

00:35:13.810 --> 00:35:16.750
We will have approximately three months for
public comments.

00:35:16.750 --> 00:35:19.510
We already had several weeks of that.

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:23.250
And I think until August 16 is the time for
the public to have its say.

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:28.240
And, at that point, the FCC’s staff, which
is dedicated, as I pointed out, they’ll

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:32.280
take stock of the facts in the record, and
then we at the appropriate time will make

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:35.550
the judgments based on those facts and the
law as we interpret it.

00:35:35.550 --> 00:35:37.320
Now, speaking of the law, you asked about
the courts.

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:42.820
And here too I think we’re guided of course
by the decisions that the courts have made.

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:47.240
And so I think it’s important to remember,
number one, that the Supreme Court in 2005

00:35:47.240 --> 00:35:50.430
explicitly blessed the path that we have proposed
to take.

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:55.810
In fact, just earlier this week, when the
DC Circuit rejected the en banc petition for

00:35:55.810 --> 00:36:00.400
review, they explicitly said, you can see
at the bottom of page four the court’s opinion,

00:36:00.400 --> 00:36:06.020
where Judges Srinivasan and Tatel recognized
that the Brand X decision stands for the proposition

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:10.170
that the agency has discretion about how to
classify internet access.

00:36:10.170 --> 00:36:14.560
And so going forward, we of course want to
be guided by these legal principles, and that’s

00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:16.760
something that’s very important to us.

00:36:16.760 --> 00:36:18.480
You also mentioned Congress.

00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:22.819
Congress, of course, has very great interest
in these issues, and that’s why I’ve been

00:36:22.819 --> 00:36:27.609
doing outreach to members of Congress on both
sides of the aisle and to both Houses of Capitol

00:36:27.609 --> 00:36:32.070
Hill to make sure that I make myself as accessible
as I can be.

00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:34.049
And people might end up disagreeing.

00:36:34.049 --> 00:36:39.039
Of course, this is a topic that I recognize
is highly fraught in political differences

00:36:39.039 --> 00:36:40.289
and policy differences.

00:36:40.289 --> 00:36:45.980
But, at the end of the day, I think that a
lot of the rhetoric obscures the basic point

00:36:45.980 --> 00:36:49.970
that I’ve been trying to make, which is
that the Clinton administration got it right

00:36:49.970 --> 00:36:52.480
in the 1990s, at the dawn of the internet
age.

00:36:52.480 --> 00:36:54.980
This is not a radical position that I’ve
been trotting out.

00:36:54.980 --> 00:36:58.799
This was the reality from the dawn of the
commercial internet until 2015.

00:36:58.799 --> 00:37:03.270
I mean, if you go back and look at President
Clinton’s statement, the internet should

00:37:03.270 --> 00:37:05.980
develop unfettered from federal and state
regulation.

00:37:05.980 --> 00:37:10.140
Go back and look at my predecessor, Chairman
Kennard’s statements, that he at the crossroads

00:37:10.140 --> 00:37:14.140
of the internet, at the beginning, he didn’t
want to dump, as he put it, the morass of

00:37:14.140 --> 00:37:16.730
Title Two on the internet type.

00:37:16.730 --> 00:37:20.210
Take a look at the bipartisan statements that
we saw from many senators, including Senators

00:37:20.210 --> 00:37:25.760
Wyden and former Senator Kerry, where they
urged the FCC not to reclassify this new technology

00:37:25.760 --> 00:37:26.859
as a Title Two service.

00:37:26.859 --> 00:37:28.369
There’s a reason why they did that.

00:37:28.369 --> 00:37:31.809
It’s because they saw the potential of this
new technology, and they didn’t want to

00:37:31.809 --> 00:37:37.089
import the legacy rules from the Ma Bell monopoly
years into this new and evolving service.

00:37:37.089 --> 00:37:40.890
And so all I’m simply saying going forward
is that — you know, I recognize that, you

00:37:40.890 --> 00:37:44.829
know, some people would prefer government
micromanagement of everything internet.

00:37:44.829 --> 00:37:47.960
Some people would prefer it to be the Wild
West, no regulation at all.

00:37:47.960 --> 00:37:49.359
My pitch is pretty simply.

00:37:49.359 --> 00:37:53.140
We go down this middle path of light-touch,
Clinton-era regulation.

00:37:53.140 --> 00:37:54.820
It’s proved to work.

00:37:54.820 --> 00:37:56.880
It’s given us $1.5 trillion in infrastructure
investment.

00:37:56.880 --> 00:37:59.640
The internet economy is the envy of the world.

00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:03.230
It’s the very re ason that some of you at
this very moment are able to tweet out your

00:38:03.230 --> 00:38:05.790
vigorous disagreement with whatever I just
said.

00:38:05.790 --> 00:38:09.799
It’s one of the great platforms I think
for democratization and entrepreneurship and

00:38:09.799 --> 00:38:10.799
engagement.

00:38:10.799 --> 00:38:12.650
Let’s just keep that going going forward.

00:38:12.650 --> 00:38:13.650
MR.

00:38:13.650 --> 00:38:15.349
EISENACH: Thank you for that.

00:38:15.349 --> 00:38:21.140
And speaking of Chairman Kennard and the Clinton
administration, Chairman Kennard’s strategic

00:38:21.140 --> 00:38:25.309
plan from 1999 talked about the growth of
competition.

00:38:25.309 --> 00:38:29.680
He said, you know, our ability to move down
the path of a less regulatory approach depends

00:38:29.680 --> 00:38:34.500
ultimately on some things occurring in terms
of — he talked specifically about whether

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:38.160
DSL would ever — you know, how that technology
would develop.

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:42.920
He talked — you know, looking forward, he
talked about I wonder how cable modem technology

00:38:42.920 --> 00:38:47.280
will ever develop and will that be really
a good way to get to the internet.

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:57.210
Another part of that competitive milieu is
the question of wireless and wireline competition.

00:38:57.210 --> 00:39:02.000
So yesterday, if I think I got this right,
the CDC reported that, for the first time

00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:06.500
there are more mobile-only telephone consumers
in households in the United States than there

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:10.910
are wired households in the United States.

00:39:10.910 --> 00:39:16.650
So my question is — I don’t think — if
this is in some particular proceeding or something

00:39:16.650 --> 00:39:18.040
you can’t answer, I understand.

00:39:18.040 --> 00:39:21.799
But just as in general, would you think at
this point that wireless is a competitor,

00:39:21.799 --> 00:39:27.140
a substitute for wireline and the FCC might
— is the Phoenix decision still law?

00:39:27.140 --> 00:39:28.140
MR.

00:39:28.140 --> 00:39:29.140
PAI: It’s a great question.

00:39:29.140 --> 00:39:32.380
I mean, to me at least, they are very competitive
offerings.

00:39:32.380 --> 00:39:36.760
If you look at how consumers are using their
mobile devices, the phone call is one of the

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:39.829
last things that people typically think about
now when they pick up a phone.

00:39:39.829 --> 00:39:41.460
And that’s a remarkable transformation.

00:39:41.460 --> 00:39:46.589
I mean, for those of us of a certain age at
least, I still remember those big, you know,

00:39:46.589 --> 00:39:49.950
phones that were hard to hold, and you’d
make a voice call and the quality was scratchy.

00:39:49.950 --> 00:39:54.359
I thought, “How is this device ever going
to become an integral part of life?”

00:39:54.359 --> 00:40:00.260
But starting with the iPhone introduction
in 2007, all the way until 2017, it’s incredible

00:40:00.260 --> 00:40:02.080
to see how we use these devices.

00:40:02.080 --> 00:40:05.880
And, you know, I look at the apps that I use
every single day.

00:40:05.880 --> 00:40:06.880
It’s amazing.

00:40:06.880 --> 00:40:12.650
From fast food to Twitter to checking my email
to, you know, reserving a flight, our iPhones

00:40:12.650 --> 00:40:15.039
are some of these platforms for innovation.

00:40:15.039 --> 00:40:18.980
And so the internet is a critical part of
that, and especially as 4G LDE gets rolled

00:40:18.980 --> 00:40:24.859
out, it’s 5G networks with gigabit three
port or even more become possible, as the

00:40:24.859 --> 00:40:28.760
next generation of Wi-Fi comes onboard so
we’re able to — for instance, free five

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:32.390
gigahertz spectrum or even 50 and 70 gigahertz
spectrum for wireless innovation.

00:40:32.390 --> 00:40:36.799
I think we’re increasingly going to see
that wireless is not just this imperfect substitute

00:40:36.799 --> 00:40:38.980
for wired connections.

00:40:38.980 --> 00:40:42.890
It is going to be the dominant means by which
people access the internet, the preferable

00:40:42.890 --> 00:40:43.890
means.

00:40:43.890 --> 00:40:48.079
And so I think the FCC needs to take stock
of that and make the appropriate policy decisions

00:40:48.079 --> 00:40:49.079
as a result.

00:40:49.079 --> 00:40:50.079
MR.

00:40:50.079 --> 00:40:51.079
EISENACH: I agree.

00:40:51.079 --> 00:40:54.220
The reference — this just occurred to me
earlier today, and I haven’t looked it up.

00:40:54.220 --> 00:41:00.549
The Phoenix decision was Cox applied for deregulation
in the Phoenix marketplace, I must say.

00:41:00.549 --> 00:41:02.480
And this was not that many years ago.

00:41:02.480 --> 00:41:08.339
And the commission’s decision was based
on the conclusion — to deny the request

00:41:08.339 --> 00:41:13.750
— was based on the conclusion that wireless
voice cell phones were not a substitute.

00:41:13.750 --> 00:41:15.730
And as far as I know, that’s still law.

00:41:15.730 --> 00:41:16.730
MR.

00:41:16.730 --> 00:41:17.730
PAI: Yeah.

00:41:17.730 --> 00:41:18.730
MR.

00:41:18.730 --> 00:41:19.730
EISENACH: Isn’t it?

00:41:19.730 --> 00:41:20.730
So maybe I’m speaking about the regulatory
underbrush.

00:41:20.730 --> 00:41:21.730
MR.

00:41:21.730 --> 00:41:22.730
PAI: I believe it is, yeah.

00:41:22.730 --> 00:41:23.730
MR.

00:41:23.730 --> 00:41:24.730
EISENACH: Maybe we could concede those.

00:41:24.730 --> 00:41:25.730
MR.

00:41:25.730 --> 00:41:26.730
PAI: Well, certainly it’s also important
to note that for the purposes of the Title

00:41:26.730 --> 00:41:30.131
Two order, I mean, the FCC had no problem
lumping wireless in with wire networks, and

00:41:30.131 --> 00:41:33.470
so, I mean, under the well-established case
of goose versus gander, I think it’s important

00:41:33.470 --> 00:41:37.940
to be consistent here and to take a holistic
view of the marketplace that’s —

00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:38.940
MR.

00:41:38.940 --> 00:41:43.940
EISENACH: I decided convergence had taken
a major step when Microsoft stopped calling

00:41:43.940 --> 00:41:51.349
this stuff on my Windows 10 operating system
programs and started calling them apps.

00:41:51.349 --> 00:41:55.289
We’ve converged.

00:41:55.289 --> 00:42:02.339
So another thing that you’ve been active
with over the course of the past 102 days

00:42:02.339 --> 00:42:06.130
is collaborating in a number of ways with
our mutual friend, Maureen Ohlhausen, at the

00:42:06.130 --> 00:42:07.130
Federal Trade Commission.

00:42:07.130 --> 00:42:12.590
So talk a little bit about the FTC-FCC collaboration
and how you see that playing out over time.

00:42:12.590 --> 00:42:13.590
MR.

00:42:13.590 --> 00:42:16.760
PAI: Chairman Ohlhausen has been a terrific
partner not just over the last 102 days, but

00:42:16.760 --> 00:42:18.420
over the last several years.

00:42:18.420 --> 00:42:23.470
And I’ve really enjoyed the chance to work
with her on some of the issues that have raised

00:42:23.470 --> 00:42:25.060
a lot of public attention.

00:42:25.060 --> 00:42:29.039
For example, privacy — she and I have consistently
said that we share the same goal here.

00:42:29.039 --> 00:42:33.539
And the goal is a consistent and comprehensive
framework that protects consumers whenever

00:42:33.539 --> 00:42:35.099
they go online.

00:42:35.099 --> 00:42:39.859
And so I have really been grateful not just
to call her a friend but also to rely on her

00:42:39.859 --> 00:42:44.480
as a resource to let us know what the FTC’s
capabilities are, what the scope of its legal

00:42:44.480 --> 00:42:48.230
authorities are, and what the best way to
secure consumer protection in the digital

00:42:48.230 --> 00:42:49.230
age is.

00:42:49.230 --> 00:42:51.570
And so going forward that’s certainly going
to be the case.

00:42:51.570 --> 00:42:54.339
I know that my staff and hers have collaborated
closely as well.

00:42:54.339 --> 00:42:58.640
And I’m really excited about the chance
to deliver more results of that sort.

00:42:58.640 --> 00:42:59.640
MR.

00:42:59.640 --> 00:43:03.760
EISENACH: Does the FCC chairman support the
end of the common carrier exemption at the

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:04.760
Federal Trade Commission?

00:43:04.760 --> 00:43:05.760
MR.

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:06.990
PAI: I think our time is coming to a close.

00:43:06.990 --> 00:43:08.160
I’m just kidding.

00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:09.869
I can’t take an official position on this.

00:43:09.869 --> 00:43:13.630
It’s a decision for Congress to make obviously
in consultation with the Federal Trade Commission,

00:43:13.630 --> 00:43:17.210
but that’s one of the areas that I know
Chairwoman Ohlhausen and even Commissioner

00:43:17.210 --> 00:43:19.630
McSweeney I believe have called for congressional
attention.

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:20.630
MR.

00:43:20.630 --> 00:43:23.599
EISENACH: I was at the FTC in the 1980s when
we were pushing for ending the common carrier

00:43:23.599 --> 00:43:24.599
exemption.

00:43:24.599 --> 00:43:26.561
So the FTC would like that.

00:43:26.561 --> 00:43:27.840
I want to stop there.

00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:31.970
We’ve got about 15 minutes left, and we
promised to have time for questions from the

00:43:31.970 --> 00:43:32.970
audience.

00:43:32.970 --> 00:43:36.319
So I’ll stop my questions there, and the
floor is open.

00:43:36.319 --> 00:43:40.140
We have microphones with staff on the sides.

00:43:40.140 --> 00:43:41.200
And so raise your hand.

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:42.200
Identify yourself.

00:43:42.200 --> 00:43:43.200
We’ll take the first one here.

00:43:43.200 --> 00:43:55.609
And wait for 
the microphone to come to you.

00:43:55.609 --> 00:43:56.609
Q: Thank you.

00:43:56.609 --> 00:43:57.609
Thank you, Chairman.

00:43:57.609 --> 00:43:58.849
Rob Colorina, AIAC Investment Group.

00:43:58.849 --> 00:44:00.500
You mentioned some time on the road.

00:44:00.500 --> 00:44:04.740
I was curious as to how you manage your travel,
what you’re thinking about outside some

00:44:04.740 --> 00:44:09.401
of the major cities and into the rural or
sort of the SME kind of territories.

00:44:09.401 --> 00:44:10.401
MR.

00:44:10.401 --> 00:44:11.401
PAI: Great question.

00:44:11.401 --> 00:44:12.401
Thanks for asking.

00:44:12.401 --> 00:44:15.760
I love traveling throughout those parts of
the country that one typically would not go

00:44:15.760 --> 00:44:17.109
as a tourist.

00:44:17.109 --> 00:44:21.269
During that same trip, for instance, I stopped
in Zelienople, Pennsylvania, population I

00:44:21.269 --> 00:44:22.269
think 3,812.

00:44:22.269 --> 00:44:27.410
Believe it or not, Zelienople is sort of the
hub in terms of internet access for a big

00:44:27.410 --> 00:44:29.119
territory in western Pennsylvania.

00:44:29.119 --> 00:44:34.490
And it was really useful for me to be able
to see that cable headend to understand how

00:44:34.490 --> 00:44:38.360
that headend was trying to deliver internet
access to hilly parts of Western PA and to

00:44:38.360 --> 00:44:43.260
see the business and economic challenges and
technical challenges that were involved in

00:44:43.260 --> 00:44:44.260
internet access.

00:44:44.260 --> 00:44:47.490
And, to be honest, those are the trips that
resonate the most with me.

00:44:47.490 --> 00:44:50.569
You’re traveling to Dillard, Nebraska, and
getting to meet Chad and Courtney Lautman,

00:44:50.569 --> 00:44:56.660
who’ve used the internet to build a beef
jerky distribution company that is shipping

00:44:56.660 --> 00:44:57.660
nationwide.

00:44:57.660 --> 00:44:58.660
It’s incredible.

00:44:58.660 --> 00:45:02.440
Going to Fort Yukon, Alaska, and getting to
see how a teacher is able to connect with

00:45:02.440 --> 00:45:06.730
her students, most of whom are Alaska natives,
in ways that were unthinkable a generation

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:07.730
ago.

00:45:07.730 --> 00:45:11.900
Visiting Carthage, Mississippi, and looking
at a gigabit fiber deployment that was never

00:45:11.900 --> 00:45:14.670
considered possible during the analogue age.

00:45:14.670 --> 00:45:18.960
Even going back to my hometown of Parsons,
Kansas, and seeing some of the challenges

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:19.960
there.

00:45:19.960 --> 00:45:24.220
My own high school, for instance, either didn’t
apply for E-Rate funding or didn’t get the

00:45:24.220 --> 00:45:27.750
funding that they needed in order to get better
digital services.

00:45:27.750 --> 00:45:32.059
And that sort of brought home to me at least
the fact that my own successors at Parsons

00:45:32.059 --> 00:45:35.920
High School weren’t necessarily getting
the same opportunity as some of their brethren

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:36.920
in bigger places.

00:45:36.920 --> 00:45:40.430
And so those are the visits that are real
important to me.

00:45:40.430 --> 00:45:41.730
I grew up in a small town.

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:43.849
I grew up in rural America.

00:45:43.849 --> 00:45:47.839
However long I may have lived in the big city,
I’m still a small town American at heart,

00:45:47.839 --> 00:45:54.039
and I’m keenly aware that the digital divide
wrecks the greatest inequality when it comes

00:45:54.039 --> 00:45:55.809
to smaller towns.

00:45:55.809 --> 00:45:59.990
And so going forward I’m going to continue
doing those visits because it helps me keep

00:45:59.990 --> 00:46:04.230
me grounded and it helps keep my eyes on the
prize here, which is that ubiquitous internet

00:46:04.230 --> 00:46:08.490
access that every American who wants it is
able to get it is ultimately something that’s

00:46:08.490 --> 00:46:10.289
going to make our nation much better off.

00:46:10.289 --> 00:46:13.869
I’d also point out, by the way, that when
I’ve had a chance to talk to my international

00:46:13.869 --> 00:46:18.440
counterparts, interestingly, this is the number-one
priority that they talk about as well.

00:46:18.440 --> 00:46:22.410
I met my counterpart in Nigeria, for example,
in February, and I was struck by the fact

00:46:22.410 --> 00:46:25.950
that he told me that 40 percent of Nigerians
lack internet access at all.

00:46:25.950 --> 00:46:30.240
And those are extremely poor rural villages
in particular.

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:34.250
A couple of years ago, when I went to India,
I sat on the outskirts of a small village

00:46:34.250 --> 00:46:39.849
in Bangalore, and I saw that internet access
for poor women was extremely important, especially

00:46:39.849 --> 00:46:44.010
over the mobile phones, because that allowed
them to get and transmit money, which allowed

00:46:44.010 --> 00:46:45.450
them to start their own businesses.

00:46:45.450 --> 00:46:49.680
In Colombia, I met some small startup entrepreneurs
from rural areas that were developing apps

00:46:49.680 --> 00:46:54.339
that allowed, for instance, fishermen to be
able to better communicate in order to assess

00:46:54.339 --> 00:46:58.490
where the fish were and try to figure out
where the marketplace opportunity was.

00:46:58.490 --> 00:47:01.480
And so this is not an American phenomenon.

00:47:01.480 --> 00:47:04.750
And the other point I’ll just stress in
closing is that when I met recently with Senator

00:47:04.750 --> 00:47:10.080
Angus King, outside of his office, he has
a wonderful poster of JFK with a quote on

00:47:10.080 --> 00:47:11.080
it.

00:47:11.080 --> 00:47:13.369
And it says, “Let us not search for the
Republican answer.

00:47:13.369 --> 00:47:15.390
Let us not search for the Democratic answer.

00:47:15.390 --> 00:47:17.170
Let us search for the right answer.”

00:47:17.170 --> 00:47:21.020
And notwithstanding the political vicissitudes
of the Open Internet Order or the other things

00:47:21.020 --> 00:47:26.250
that occupy the headlines, at the end of the
day, I remain optimistic, perhaps foolishly

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:30.079
so, that internet access is something we can
all rally around, that all of us are better

00:47:30.079 --> 00:47:34.000
off as Americans when we focus on that prize.

00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:38.540
And rural America, at least to me, is squarely
in the heart of that prize.

00:47:38.540 --> 00:47:39.540
MR.

00:47:39.540 --> 00:47:41.630
EISENACH: Other questions.

00:47:41.630 --> 00:47:45.670
I see one in the back of the room here.

00:47:45.670 --> 00:47:49.059
Q: Steven Nyikos.

00:47:49.059 --> 00:47:53.640
I’m with the Carnegie Endowment next door.

00:47:53.640 --> 00:47:54.640
MR.

00:47:54.640 --> 00:47:55.640
PAI: Hi.

00:47:55.640 --> 00:48:01.240
Q: I’m wondering — one of the greatest
ways to foster low-cost and efficient internet

00:48:01.240 --> 00:48:05.190
access in new areas is going to be through
fostering competition.

00:48:05.190 --> 00:48:11.319
Can you talk about measures that you might
— the FCC might be engaged in to foster

00:48:11.319 --> 00:48:13.440
ISP competition specifically?

00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:16.970
And with the light touch that you’re talking
about from the Clinton era, means of modernizing

00:48:16.970 --> 00:48:25.170
and ensuring that that is an equal playing
field for startup or smaller ISPs and the

00:48:25.170 --> 00:48:26.529
larger ISP companies.

00:48:26.529 --> 00:48:27.529
MR.

00:48:27.529 --> 00:48:29.269
PAI: Fantastic question.

00:48:29.269 --> 00:48:31.299
So here we’ve been extremely aggressive.

00:48:31.299 --> 00:48:36.700
As you heard during the speech, for instance
— also we have two basic tools in the toolbox.

00:48:36.700 --> 00:48:41.430
One is the wise stewardship of the federal
subsidy programs that the FCC oversees.

00:48:41.430 --> 00:48:45.290
And the other is the regulatory framework,
the rules of the road that we set for the

00:48:45.290 --> 00:48:46.320
private sector.

00:48:46.320 --> 00:48:50.430
So with respect to federal subsidies, the
bottom-line philosophy that I’ve tried to

00:48:50.430 --> 00:48:54.880
apply to these programs, which I’m glad
my colleagues have supported, is making sure

00:48:54.880 --> 00:48:58.520
that we redirect funding to the areas that
are unserved, to Americans who are off the

00:48:58.520 --> 00:49:03.589
grid altogether, and make sure they are devoted
to carriers who are committed and have been

00:49:03.589 --> 00:49:07.589
proven to be committed to developing broadband
networks in those areas.

00:49:07.589 --> 00:49:11.260
And these are not the big companies by definition
because you don’t see as big of a return

00:49:11.260 --> 00:49:12.510
on the investment in these areas.

00:49:12.510 --> 00:49:16.980
These are some of the smaller companies that
are taking these funds and in a fiscally responsible

00:49:16.980 --> 00:49:19.650
way applying them to close that digital divide.

00:49:19.650 --> 00:49:25.279
Additionally, we’ve also taken a number
of other universal service actions along those

00:49:25.279 --> 00:49:26.279
lines.

00:49:26.279 --> 00:49:29.339
Just last month, for instance, at our meeting,
we took a very arcane step, which I mentioned

00:49:29.339 --> 00:49:34.049
briefly, to change our CAPEX rules, which
inadvertently stood in the way of companies

00:49:34.049 --> 00:49:38.660
wanting to deliver high-speed access to some
of the highest-cost areas within their territory.

00:49:38.660 --> 00:49:44.420
And that was perversely a way of essentially
redlining off entire rural areas from broadband

00:49:44.420 --> 00:49:45.440
deployment.

00:49:45.440 --> 00:49:49.599
So the second major way we want to incentivize
competition is through adjusting our rules.

00:49:49.599 --> 00:49:54.410
And here I’m really excited in particular
about the impact of small wireless and wired

00:49:54.410 --> 00:49:55.420
broadband competitors.

00:49:55.420 --> 00:49:58.829
On the wireless side, for instance, I’ve
consistently said that one of the reasons

00:49:58.829 --> 00:50:03.359
I believe so much in five gigahertz spectrum
is that could be used by fixed wireless providers

00:50:03.359 --> 00:50:06.470
to be a competitive alternative to their wired
brethren.

00:50:06.470 --> 00:50:10.839
And so if we were able, for instance, to get
five gigahertz spectrum that involves very

00:50:10.839 --> 00:50:15.400
wide channels, then they can supply very high
speeds to competitors.

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:19.049
It would be essentially comparable, hopefully,
to the fixed broadband networks.

00:50:19.049 --> 00:50:22.990
And especially if we don’t need to deploy
all of that fixed infrastructure that you

00:50:22.990 --> 00:50:23.990
would on the wired side.

00:50:23.990 --> 00:50:26.589
That could be a pretty important competitors,
especially in rural areas.

00:50:26.589 --> 00:50:31.839
With respect to the wired competitors too,
we’ve made sure that we take steps to relieve

00:50:31.839 --> 00:50:34.849
some of the regulatory burdens, to make it
easier for them to build a business case so

00:50:34.849 --> 00:50:36.160
they can compete with the big guys.

00:50:36.160 --> 00:50:41.079
So, for example, the creation of the Broadband
Deployment Advisory Committee, the BDAC — they’re

00:50:41.079 --> 00:50:46.589
targeting ways to make it easier for the smaller
competitors to gain access to poles, to make

00:50:46.589 --> 00:50:50.329
is cheaper for them to attach equipment to
poles, for them to be able to get access to

00:50:50.329 --> 00:50:54.099
the conduit that’s in the ground, or for
them to get quicker state and local approval

00:50:54.099 --> 00:50:55.589
for some of these deployments.

00:50:55.589 --> 00:51:00.230
If you think about it, these regulatory barriers,
they affect everybody who wants to be an ISP,

00:51:00.230 --> 00:51:01.950
but especially the smaller competitors.

00:51:01.950 --> 00:51:03.920
And just to give you one quick example of
that.

00:51:03.920 --> 00:51:09.730
I visited with a company called Rocket Fiber,
small ISP, a startup ISP in Detroit, Michigan.

00:51:09.730 --> 00:51:12.680
And they told me, when they’ve initially
tried to go to the city of Detroit to get

00:51:12.680 --> 00:51:17.410
access to the city-owned poles, Detroit quoted
them a price of something like I want to say

00:51:17.410 --> 00:51:20.880
$3,000, maybe even $5,000 for each and every
poll.

00:51:20.880 --> 00:51:26.480
And if you’re a small ISP, I mean, come
on, in a city as densely populated as Detroit,

00:51:26.480 --> 00:51:27.700
that price is prohibitive.

00:51:27.700 --> 00:51:32.859
But negotiating down the price, they were
able to get access at a reasonable cost to

00:51:32.859 --> 00:51:33.859
them.

00:51:33.859 --> 00:51:34.930
And not every ISP is so lucky.

00:51:34.930 --> 00:51:38.319
And so we want to reduce that cost element
in order to help those smaller competitors.

00:51:38.319 --> 00:51:43.190
And moving forward, last September, I proposed
my digital empowerment agenda.

00:51:43.190 --> 00:51:46.660
One critical part of that was for Congress
to give the FCC additional authority in this

00:51:46.660 --> 00:51:51.240
area — help us create, for example, gigabit
opportunity zones, which would allow us to

00:51:51.240 --> 00:51:57.640
designate either large rural counties or even
small urban areas as 75 percent or less of

00:51:57.640 --> 00:51:59.119
the national median income.

00:51:59.119 --> 00:52:02.789
And in those areas, we provide tax incentives
to these small companies to provide a competitive

00:52:02.789 --> 00:52:03.789
alternative.

00:52:03.789 --> 00:52:08.049
I’m thrilled to see Senator Capito recently
introduce legislation just this week on that

00:52:08.049 --> 00:52:09.049
very score.

00:52:09.049 --> 00:52:13.059
Similarly, we would love for Congress to consider
giving us additional authority over utility

00:52:13.059 --> 00:52:17.160
poles that are — or poles that are owned
by governments and railroads.

00:52:17.160 --> 00:52:20.150
Very arcane, it’s never going to make the
front page of any newspaper, but given the

00:52:20.150 --> 00:52:23.880
fact that this is a major cost element for
smaller competitive ISPs that could really

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:33.220
help us attack that competition equation in
a way that will be good for consumers in the

00:52:33.220 --> 00:52:34.299
long run.

00:52:34.299 --> 00:52:35.299
MR.

00:52:35.299 --> 00:52:37.540
EISENACH: Should we go here?

00:52:37.540 --> 00:52:39.710
One on this side?

00:52:39.710 --> 00:52:40.790
Go ahead.

00:52:40.790 --> 00:52:41.869
Q: Hi.

00:52:41.869 --> 00:52:43.839
Bill Signer with the Carmen Group.

00:52:43.839 --> 00:52:44.839
MR.

00:52:44.839 --> 00:52:45.839
PAI: Hi.

00:52:45.839 --> 00:52:47.290
Q: First, thank you for coming and speaking
today.

00:52:47.290 --> 00:52:51.339
Your comments are personal about the robocalls.

00:52:51.339 --> 00:52:54.609
Whatever you do about that is great because
I got one while we were sitting here.

00:52:54.609 --> 00:52:55.609
(Laughter.)

00:52:55.609 --> 00:52:56.609
MR.

00:52:56.609 --> 00:52:57.609
PAI: I hope you didn’t pay up.

00:52:57.609 --> 00:53:02.630
Q: I did not, but I will tell you that one
of my thoughts about that is you should be

00:53:02.630 --> 00:53:08.110
going after the people who benefit — not
the companies that are doing the calls, but

00:53:08.110 --> 00:53:14.049
the end of beneficiaries of that so you could
be fining them and I think that would be great.

00:53:14.049 --> 00:53:18.160
What I want to talk a little bit about is
I work with the burglar and fire alarm industry.

00:53:18.160 --> 00:53:19.160
MR.

00:53:19.160 --> 00:53:20.160
PAI: OK.

00:53:20.160 --> 00:53:26.720
Q: Very highly competitive industry, overwhelmingly
small business and involved in public safety.

00:53:26.720 --> 00:53:33.369
We’re concerned that the common carriers,
when they become competitors of ours, that

00:53:33.369 --> 00:53:38.420
basically they use that control over the network
to get a competitive advantage.

00:53:38.420 --> 00:53:42.720
You may be aware there are provisions in the
law that protect us.

00:53:42.720 --> 00:53:47.619
We are concerned that — and this is not
a net neutrality issue.

00:53:47.619 --> 00:53:52.700
What we’re concerned about is if things
in Title Two, which is where we are, go over

00:53:52.700 --> 00:53:58.940
to the Federal Trade Commission, by the time
we get heard, a lot of our small businesses

00:53:58.940 --> 00:54:00.680
are going to be put out of business.

00:54:00.680 --> 00:54:07.779
So the two things we would like to see happen
is, one, continuation under FCC jurisdiction

00:54:07.779 --> 00:54:14.440
for at least the alarm complaints and issues,
and, two, making sure — well, that’s basically

00:54:14.440 --> 00:54:16.329
the major thing we’re concerned about.

00:54:16.329 --> 00:54:17.329
MR.

00:54:17.329 --> 00:54:18.329
PAI: OK.

00:54:18.329 --> 00:54:19.329
Thanks for flagging that concern.

00:54:19.329 --> 00:54:22.450
It’s been a while since I took a look at
the legal framework surrounding these issues,

00:54:22.450 --> 00:54:23.721
but I’d be happy consider it.

00:54:23.721 --> 00:54:29.119
In the meantime, please do file either in
an existing proceeding or if there is a petition

00:54:29.119 --> 00:54:30.119
or application —

00:54:30.119 --> 00:54:41.609
Q: The second part of it is because it only
applies basically to landlines, we want to

00:54:41.609 --> 00:54:42.609
be modernized, and I’m sure it applies to
the internet service providers.

00:54:42.609 --> 00:54:43.609
MR.

00:54:43.609 --> 00:54:44.609
PAI: OK.

00:54:44.609 --> 00:54:45.609
Like I said, I’d be happy to take a look.

00:54:45.609 --> 00:54:46.609
Send some paper our way, and I’ll do my
best to carve out some time to take a look

00:54:46.609 --> 00:54:47.609
at it.

00:54:47.609 --> 00:54:48.609
MR.

00:54:48.609 --> 00:54:52.260
EISENACH: I think we have time for one more
question probably.

00:54:52.260 --> 00:54:53.690
We’ve had the left.

00:54:53.690 --> 00:54:54.900
It’s time for the right.

00:54:54.900 --> 00:54:56.010
Right, left.

00:54:56.010 --> 00:54:59.619
I got one right back here.

00:54:59.619 --> 00:55:03.460
Yes, sir.

00:55:03.460 --> 00:55:13.069
Q: David Hatch with dealReporter.

00:55:13.069 --> 00:55:20.769
So the previous FCC did not look favorably
at reducing the number of nationwide wireless

00:55:20.769 --> 00:55:22.839
carriers from four to three.

00:55:22.839 --> 00:55:26.819
And given the statements that you just made
about the level of competition, is that a

00:55:26.819 --> 00:55:29.490
possibility that you’d be open to?

00:55:29.490 --> 00:55:30.490
MR.

00:55:30.490 --> 00:55:36.890
PAI: I take the role of the — my approach
to the regulatory role is one of humility

00:55:36.890 --> 00:55:42.049
— that I don’t claim to know in a vacuum
what the optimal market structure of any particular

00:55:42.049 --> 00:55:45.720
marketplace is, what the right number of competitors
should be.

00:55:45.720 --> 00:55:50.660
Our goal is always to make sure that the marketplace
functions in a way that benefits the public

00:55:50.660 --> 00:55:51.660
interest.

00:55:51.660 --> 00:55:56.950
And so to me at least, we take a look at the
marketplace as it stands, try to figure out

00:55:56.950 --> 00:56:02.440
is there competition, are prices going down,
is application and service innovation going

00:56:02.440 --> 00:56:05.599
up, those kinds of things, and then make the
appropriate judgment.

00:56:05.599 --> 00:56:09.059
For instance, through our wireless competition
report — if any transaction is presented

00:56:09.059 --> 00:56:13.400
to us, the framework I’ve applied and I’ve
consistently applied going back to my statements

00:56:13.400 --> 00:56:18.289
on November 30, 2011, when I was a nominee,
was that we always evaluate transactions based

00:56:18.289 --> 00:56:19.789
on the public interest standard.

00:56:19.789 --> 00:56:23.200
Figuring out whether the consummation of that
transaction would be good for competition

00:56:23.200 --> 00:56:27.910
or consumers and to make the appropriate judgment
thereafter.

00:56:27.910 --> 00:56:32.170
That’s the framework I’m going to apply
going forward if we’re presented with any

00:56:32.170 --> 00:56:33.170
transaction where (it is ?).

00:56:33.170 --> 00:56:34.170
MR.

00:56:34.170 --> 00:56:35.250
EISENACH: I’m told we’ll take one more.

00:56:35.250 --> 00:56:39.650
And I saw one more hand over here.

00:56:39.650 --> 00:56:40.650
One more here.

00:56:40.650 --> 00:56:41.650
Yes.

00:56:41.650 --> 00:56:42.650
Here we go.

00:56:42.650 --> 00:56:43.650
MR.

00:56:43.650 --> 00:56:47.329
PAI: Behind the fellow with the stylish fedora.

00:56:47.329 --> 00:56:48.329
Q: Yeah.

00:56:48.329 --> 00:56:49.660
Marne Bax (ph) with “Morning Consult.”

00:56:49.660 --> 00:56:55.200
I was just wondering if you could talk a little
bit more about — I don’t know if this

00:56:55.200 --> 00:57:00.900
is related to the federal subsidy you were
talking about earlier, but Commissioner Clyburn

00:57:00.900 --> 00:57:06.950
had suggested that the way that the universal
service fee is funded right now is unsustainable

00:57:06.950 --> 00:57:09.210
since it’s coming mostly from landline phones.

00:57:09.210 --> 00:57:13.460
Can you just talk a little bit about that
and how federal subsidies will be collected

00:57:13.460 --> 00:57:16.710
in the future if there are changes to that.

00:57:16.710 --> 00:57:17.710
MR.

00:57:17.710 --> 00:57:18.710
PAI: Sure.

00:57:18.710 --> 00:57:23.599
So to the folks who are blissfully unfamiliar
with this area, the universal service fund

00:57:23.599 --> 00:57:28.030
is funded by essentially a tax that is on
every phone bill the consumer pays.

00:57:28.030 --> 00:57:32.809
And so if you look at your bill, you see universal
service fee, those fees go into essentially

00:57:32.809 --> 00:57:35.079
a pot of money that the FCC oversees.

00:57:35.079 --> 00:57:38.730
There are four different universal service
and programs, one of which I mentioned today:

00:57:38.730 --> 00:57:44.170
the high-cost program for delivering broadband
to rural American, an unserved America.

00:57:44.170 --> 00:57:48.999
So, yeah, that fee is something that varies
over time.

00:57:48.999 --> 00:57:56.329
The FCC and the company that oversees for
administering this program make a determination

00:57:56.329 --> 00:57:59.550
about what that contribution factor is or
should be.

00:57:59.550 --> 00:58:04.650
And there are existing bodies to determine,
you know, whether the fee should be adjusted.

00:58:04.650 --> 00:58:09.450
Right now, my colleague Commissioner O’Rielly
is the chair of what’s called a joint board

00:58:09.450 --> 00:58:11.519
that oversees these issues.

00:58:11.519 --> 00:58:13.950
And they’re squarely tackling that question.

00:58:13.950 --> 00:58:18.360
So we haven’t — the discussion has been
ongoing for quite a while, and we haven’t

00:58:18.360 --> 00:58:20.089
made any determinations yet.

00:58:20.089 --> 00:58:25.470
So I hate to pass the buck to my good friend,
but since he’s the chairman of that relevant

00:58:25.470 --> 00:58:32.319
joint board, I have to defer answering — you’re
free to talk to him if you like about that

00:58:32.319 --> 00:58:33.319
question.

00:58:33.319 --> 00:58:34.319
MR.

00:58:34.319 --> 00:58:35.319
EISENACH: Process reform in action.

00:58:35.319 --> 00:58:36.319
MR.

00:58:36.319 --> 00:58:37.319
PAI: I know.

00:58:37.319 --> 00:58:38.420
I feel bad imposing homework on my colleagues.

00:58:38.420 --> 00:58:39.420
MR.

00:58:39.420 --> 00:58:41.829
EISENACH: Ajit, it’s been wonderful having
you here.

00:58:41.829 --> 00:58:45.460
You learn something every time — I always
learn something when I listen to you.

00:58:45.460 --> 00:58:48.930
Today I learned something I didn’t expect
that you’d spent so much time in prison.

00:58:48.930 --> 00:58:49.930
(Laughter.)

00:58:49.930 --> 00:58:53.849
So it’s — it’s not on your CV, but thanks
for letting us know.

00:58:53.849 --> 00:58:54.849
MR.

00:58:54.849 --> 00:58:55.950
PAI: Voluntarily I hasten to add, for those
watching at home.

00:58:55.950 --> 00:58:56.950
Yeah.

00:58:56.950 --> 00:58:57.950
MR.

00:58:57.950 --> 00:58:59.141
EISENACH: Listen, thank you so much for being
here.

00:58:59.141 --> 00:59:01.029
I ask everyone to join me in thanking Ajit.

00:59:01.029 --> 00:59:06.630
Also stay seated as we’re leaving the room.

00:59:06.630 --> 00:59:07.319
(Applause.)

